{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","count":17,"questions":[{"id":52007,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In the circuit shown below, all the inductors (assumed ideal) and resistors are identical. The current through the resistance on the right is I after the key $\\mathrm{K}$ has been switched on for a long time. The currents through the three resistors(in order, from left to right) immediately after the key is switched off are\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FdG4jrp-FhLTS50SVcha3KaMVkqSQRkriLTp_5EVY6d8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FdG4jrp-FhLTS50SVcha3KaMVkqSQRkriLTp_5EVY6d8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FdG4jrp-FhLTS50SVcha3KaMVkqSQRkriLTp_5EVY6d8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$2 I$ upwards, $I$ downwards and $I$ downwards.","$2 I$ downwards, $I$ downwards and $I$ downwards.","$I$ downwards, $I$ downwards and $I$ downwards","$0, I$ downwards and $I$ downwards"],"answer":0,"solution":"As current does not change through inductor immediately. It remains I through right \\& middle resistance \\& 2 i upwards in left resistance?","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21310a3203c4ff1d9db"},{"id":52008,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An ac voltmeter connected between points $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ in the circuit below reads $36 \\mathrm{~V}$. If it is connected between $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$, the reading is $39 \\mathrm{~V}$. The reading when it is connected between $\\mathrm{B}$ and $\\mathrm{D}$ is $25 \\mathrm{~V}$. What will the voltmeter read when it is connected between A and D?) Assume that the voltmeter reads true rms voltage values and that the source generated a pure ac.)\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/kvpy/4p650nUQlljfwBY3oaLeVUD9_cLmPHfh8XFULYQzuhM.original.fullsize.png\">","options":["$\\sqrt{481} \\mathrm{~V}$","31V","61V","$\\sqrt{3361} \\mathrm{~V}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b01c9b8ff4e5ec7c","question":"An ac voltmeter connected between points $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ in the circuit below reads $36 \\mathrm{~V}$. If it is connected between $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$, the reading is $39 \\mathrm{~V}$. The reading when it is connected between $\\mathrm{B}$ and $\\mathrm{D}$ is $25 \\mathrm{~V}$. What will the voltmeter read when it is connected between A and D?) Assume that the voltmeter reads true rms voltage values and that the source generated a pure ac.)\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/kvpy/4p650nUQlljfwBY3oaLeVUD9_cLmPHfh8XFULYQzuhM.original.fullsize.png\">","explanation":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FAGq4vRdaB5sGfpatX3wnrSkwLCOX2NAFOIQ1UZmiwKM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"},{"id":52009,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A blackbox (BB) which may contain a combination of electrical circuit elements (resistor, capacitor or inductor) is connected with other external circuit elements as shown below in the figure (a). After the switch (S) is closed at time $t=0$, the current (I) as a function of time $(t)$ is shown in the figure (b).\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FLkZqTDHyUBNh2rCULtJH_djf0YpVrTfQe5EMtmdb1Gs.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLkZqTDHyUBNh2rCULtJH_djf0YpVrTfQe5EMtmdb1Gs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLkZqTDHyUBNh2rCULtJH_djf0YpVrTfQe5EMtmdb1Gs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nFrom this we can infer that the blackbox contains","options":["A resistor and a capacitor in series","A resistor and a capacitor in parallel","A resistor and an inductor in series","A resistor and an inductor in parallel"],"answer":2,"solution":"I - t graph is for L-R series circuit.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2138f53d09b6093f1b8"},{"id":52010,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Consider the R-L-C circuit given below. The circuit is driven by a $50 \\mathrm{ Hz}$ AC source with peak voltage $220 \\mathrm{ V}.$ If $\\mathrm{R}=400 \\Omega, \\mathrm{C}=200 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$ and $\\mathrm{L}=6 \\mathrm{H}$, the maximum current in the circuit is closest to\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FooYmQ-JCWGYzU8ccOvLtXaJkvot4wxxXJLPL1Giy3Hs.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FooYmQ-JCWGYzU8ccOvLtXaJkvot4wxxXJLPL1Giy3Hs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FooYmQ-JCWGYzU8ccOvLtXaJkvot4wxxXJLPL1Giy3Hs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$0.120 \\mathrm{ A}$","$0.55 \\mathrm{ A}$","$1.2 \\mathrm{ A}$","$5.5 \\mathrm{ A}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L}}=\\omega \\mathrm{L}=2 \\pi \\times 50 \\times 6=600 \\pi \\Omega\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}=\\frac{1}{2 \\pi 80 \\times 200 \\times 10^{-6}}=\\frac{100}{2 \\pi}=\\frac{50}{\\pi} \\Omega\\mathrm{I}_{\\max }=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{z}}=\\frac{220}{\\sqrt{\\mathrmR^{2}+\\left(\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L}}-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{C}}\\right)^{2}}}=\\frac{220}{\\sqrt{400^{2}+\\left(600 \\pi-\\frac{50}{\\pi}\\right)^{2}}}\\mathrm{I}_{\\max }=0.120 \\mathrm{ A}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2132095493debcb5d89"},{"id":52011,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The primary and the secondary coils of a transformer contain 10 and 100 turns, respectively. The primary coil is connected to a battery that supplies a constant voltage of $1.5$ volts. the voltage across the secondary coil is","options":["$1.5 \\mathrm{ V}$","$0.15 \\mathrm{ V}$","$0.0 \\mathrm{ V}$","$15 \\mathrm{ V}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Since the voltage production is based upon A.C. supply and this voltage is D.C which is constant. Therefore, no flux will change in secondary and no voltage will be induced.\nAnswer is (C) $0 \\mathrm{ V}$.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2134b631410fa3b2a76"},{"id":52012,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The output voltage (taken across the resistance) of a $LCR$ series resonant circuit falls to half its peak value at a frequency of $200$ Hz and again reaches the same value at $800Hz.$& The bandwidth of this circuit is:","options":["$200$ Hz","$200\\sqrt{3}Hz$","$400$ Hz","$600$ Hz"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>${V}_{Output&#160;} = {V}_{R}$</p> <p>$= {i}_{rms}R$</p> <p>$= \\dfrac{{V}_{0}R}{Z} = \\dfrac{{V}_{0}R}{R^{2} + \\left({X}_{L} - {X}_{C}\\right)2}$</p> <p>For peak ${X}_{L} = {X}_{C} ⇒ {V}_{peak&#160;} = {V}_{0}$<br />\nFor ${V}_{Output&#160;} = \\dfrac{V0}{2}$<br />\n$\\dfrac{V0}{2} = \\dfrac{{V}_{0}R}{R^{2} + \\left({X}_{L} - {X}_{C}\\right)2}$<br />\n$R^{2} + \\left({X}_{L} - {X}_{C}\\right)2 = 4R^{2}$<br />\n${X}_{L} - {X}_{C} = \\pm \\sqrt{3}R$<br />\n$\\omegaL - \\dfrac{1}{\\omegaC} = \\pm \\sqrt{3}R$<br />\n${&#969;}^{2}LC ∓ \\sqrt{3}R\\omegaC - 1 = 0$</p> <p>$\\omega = \\dfrac{\\pm \\sqrt{3}RC \\pm \\sqrt{3R^{2}C^{2} + 4LC}}{2LC}$<br />\n${&#969;}_{1} = \\dfrac{- \\sqrt{3}RC + \\sqrt{3R^{2}C^{2} + 4LC}}{2LC} = 200 \\times 2\\pi$<br />\n${&#969;}_{2} = \\dfrac{+ \\sqrt{3}RC + \\sqrt{3R^{2}C^{2} + 4LC}}{2LC} = 800 \\times 2\\pi$<br />\n${&#969;}_{2} - {&#969;}_{1} = 600 \\times 2\\pi = \\sqrt{3}\\dfrac{R}{L}$<br />\nBandwidth $= \\dfrac{R}{L} = \\dfrac{2\\pi \\times 600}{3}$<br />\n$\\Deltaf = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{R}{L} = \\dfrac{600}{3} = 200\\sqrt{3}$</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da753865bdc305a8de462"},{"id":52013,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An initially uncharged capacitor $C$ is being charged by a battery of emf $E$ through a resistance $R$ upto the instant when the capacitor is charged to the potential $E / 2,$& the ratio of the work done by the battery to the heat dissipated by the resistor is given by:-","options":["$2: 1$","$3: 1$","$4: 3$","$4: 1$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/kvpy/0c916468-295e-4835-bb40-08a5dcc3944b-image.png\" style=\"width: 220px; height: 176px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>$i = \\dfrac{E}{R}{e}^{- t / RC}, Q = CE\\left(1 - {e}^{- t / RC}\\right)$<br />\nCapacitor is charged to $\\dfrac{E}{2}$<br />\nSo $Q = \\dfrac{CE}{2}$<br />\n$∴ \\dfrac{CE}{2} = CE\\left(1 - {e}^{- t / RC}\\right)$<br />\n$\\dfrac{1}{2} = {e}^{- t / RC}$<br />\n$t = RCln2$<br />\nWork done by battery $= \\left({Q}_{\\text{flown}}\\right) ( \\DeltaV )$</p>\n\n<p>$= \\left(\\dfrac{CE}{2}\\right) ( E ) = \\dfrac{C{E}^{2}}{2}$</p>\n\n<p>Heat dissipated $= {∫}_{0}^{RCℓn2}{i}^{2}Rdt$<br />\n$= \\dfrac{{E}^{2}}{R}{∫}_{0}^{RCln2}{e}^{- 2t / RC} \\cdot dt$<br />\n$= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{C{E}^{2}}{2}\\right)$<br />\n$\\dfrac{\\text{Work done}}{\\text{Heat dissipated}} = \\dfrac{C{E}^{2} / 2}{\\dfrac{3}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{C{E}^{2}}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{4}{3}$</p>\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da75304e00918c6c9bd61"},{"id":52014,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A circuit consists of a coil with inductance $L$ and an uncharged capacitor of capacitance $C$. The coil is in a constant uniform magnetic field such that the flux through the coil is $ϕ$. At time $t = 0min$, the magnetic field is abruptly switched OFF. Let ${&#969;}_{0} = 1 / \\sqrt{LC}$ and ignore the resistance of the circuit. Then,","options":["current in the circuit is $I\\left(t\\right) = \\left(ϕ / L\\right)cos{&#969;}_{0}t$","magnitude of the charge on the capacitor is $| Q\\left(t\\right) | = 2C{&#969;}_{0}\\left|sin{&#969;}_{0}t\\right|$","initial current in the circuit is infinite","initial charge on the capacitor is $C{&#969;}_{0}ϕ$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>At $t = 0$ capacitor is uncharged and flux of inductor is $ϕ$.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F0813edc3-3349-4564-aaa9-78bce3a9ee20-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F0813edc3-3349-4564-aaa9-78bce3a9ee20-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F0813edc3-3349-4564-aaa9-78bce3a9ee20-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Now, using $ϕ = LI$, at $t = 0$ current in circuit is</p>\n<p>${I}_{0} = \\dfrac{ϕ}{L}$</p>\n<p>Instantaneous current in circuit is $\\dfrac{dq}{dt}$.</p>\n<p>where, $q$ is solution of $L\\dfrac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + \\dfrac{q}{C} = 0$</p>\n<p>or $\\dfrac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + \\dfrac{q}{LC} = 0$</p>\n<p>At $t = 0$, current in circuit is non-zero, so instantaneous current is given by </p>\n<p>$= {I}_{0}cos\\omegat = \\dfrac{ϕ}{L} \\cdot cos\\omegat$</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da72147b509e854ecf28d"},{"id":52015,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The circuit shown has been connected for a long time. The voltage across the capacitor is\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FmWgxuSawr9caEpT3U2FuHHnnf9hFlKOadaJ-62BgK6U.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FmWgxuSawr9caEpT3U2FuHHnnf9hFlKOadaJ-62BgK6U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FmWgxuSawr9caEpT3U2FuHHnnf9hFlKOadaJ-62BgK6U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$1.2 \\mathrm{ V}$","$2.0 \\mathrm{ V}$","$2.4 \\mathrm{ V}$","$4.0 \\mathrm{ V}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"In stready state i through capacitor is zero. Hence $V$ across $2 k \\Omega=V$ across capacitor Vacross $2 \\mathrm{k} \\Omega=\\frac{2}{2+1} \\times 6=4 \\mathrm{ V}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213f05571ee01ed20cb"},{"id":52016,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"For a domestic AC supply of $220 \\mathrm{ V}$ at 50 cycles per second, the potential difference between the terminals of a two pin electric outlet in a room is given by -","options":["$V(t)=220 \\sqrt{2} \\cos (100 \\pi t)$","$\\mathrm{V}(\\mathrm{t})=220 \\cos (50 \\mathrm{t})$","$\\mathrm{V}(\\mathrm{t})=220 \\cos (100 \\pi \\mathrm{t})$","$V(t)=220 \\sqrt{2} \\cos (50 \\mathrm{t})$"],"answer":0,"solution":"R.M.S. value $=220 \\mathrm{ V}$\nPeak value $=220 \\sqrt{2}\\omega=2 \\pi \\mathrm{n}=2 \\pi \\times 50=100 \\piV(t)=220 \\sqrt{2} \\cos (100 \\pi t)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213c077cc4a0327c894"},{"id":52017,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Consider the infinite ladder circuit shown below.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FzhEcriButIOAnbIulWAjJTv0tn2Bb0YjCXuIRXhXeL0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FzhEcriButIOAnbIulWAjJTv0tn2Bb0YjCXuIRXhXeL0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FzhEcriButIOAnbIulWAjJTv0tn2Bb0YjCXuIRXhXeL0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nFor which angular frequency $\\omega$ will the circuit behave like a pure inductance?","options":["$\\frac{\\mathrm{LC}}{\\sqrt{2}}$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{LC}}}$","$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{L C}}$","$\\frac{2 \\mathrm{ L}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{C}}}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Let the equivalent impedance of the circuit be $Z$\nSo $Z=\\omega L+Z^{\\prime}$\nnow $Z^{\\prime}=\\frac{Z X_{C}}{Z+X_{C}}$\n\\therefore Z=$\\omega$ L+$\\left(\\frac{Z \\times \\frac{1}{\\omega C}}{Z+\\frac{1}{\\omega C}}\\right)$ on ving we get $Z=\\frac{\\omega L C \\pm \\sqrt{(\\omega L C)^{2}-4 L C}}{2 C}$\nfor $Z$ to be purely inductive\n\n$\\omega^{2}$ L^{2} C^{2}-4 L C=0 $\\text{$ or $} \\omega=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{L C}}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21349b82807a3896965"},{"id":52018,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The circuit shown consists of a switch (S), a battery (B) of emf E, a resistance $\\mathrm{R}$, and an inductor $\\mathrm{L}$.\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/kvpy/SklU_8IF8cEZ6EwJ1QHJgdmxE2Gh02UJ5_inSIt5tYk.original.fullsize.png\"><br>\nThe current in the circuit at the instant the switch is closed is-","options":["$\\mathrm{E} / \\mathrm{R}$","$E / R(1-e)$","$\\infty$","0"],"answer":3,"solution":"Using the equation $\\mathrm{I}=\\mathrm{I}_{0}\\left(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-\\frac{t}{t_{L}}}\\right)$\nPut $\\mathrm{t}=0$ we get $\\mathrm{I}=0$\nJust when the battery is closed inductor provides infinite resistance to the current flow\n$\\therefore$ current is zero initially.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131097d942b27e98ce"},{"id":52019,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Alternating Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In the circuit shown, the switch is closed at time $\\mathrm{t}=0$.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FPJcQibhAIYpLWyNexftntDZlO6SxwpWwCUs9RiV2qy0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FPJcQibhAIYpLWyNexftntDZlO6SxwpWwCUs9RiV2qy0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FPJcQibhAIYpLWyNexftntDZlO6SxwpWwCUs9RiV2qy0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nWhich of the graphs shown below best represents the voltage across the inductor, as seen on an oscilloscope ?<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYDUfMCoyY6Y6j9PzcjsLyyXHWUsT4oJ9WES1owgwpcA.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYDUfMCoyY6Y6j9PzcjsLyyXHWUsT4oJ9WES1owgwpcA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storag